### gsap.matchMedia()

Source: https://gsap.com/docs/v3/GSAP/gsap.matchMedia%28%29

Creates a MatchMedia object that can be used to add media queries and manage animations/ScrollTriggers that respond to viewport changes.

```APIDOC
## gsap.matchMedia()

### Description
Creates a MatchMedia object that allows you to add media queries and associate handler functions. When a media query matches, the handler function runs, and any GSAP animations or ScrollTriggers created inside are collected for later reversion.

### Method
`gsap.matchMedia()`

### Syntax
```js
let mm = gsap.matchMedia();
```

### Parameters
None.

### Returns
A MatchMedia object with `.add()` and `.revert()` methods.

### Example
```js
let mm = gsap.matchMedia();
```

### .add() Method

#### Description
Adds a media query and a handler function. When the media query matches, the handler runs. All GSAP animations and ScrollTriggers created inside the handler are collected and can be reverted when the condition stops matching.

#### Syntax
```js
mm.add(query, handler, scope?);
```

#### Parameters
- **query** (string | object) - Required - A media query string (e.g., `"(min-width: 800px)"`) or a conditions object with arbitrarily-named query strings.
- **handler** (function) - Required - The function to invoke when the media query matches. GSAP animations and ScrollTriggers created inside are collected for reversion.
- **scope** (Element | React Ref | Angular ElementRef) - Optional - Scopes all GSAP selector text inside the handler to this element or ref, so only its descendants can be selected.

#### Example
```js
mm.add("(min-width: 800px)", () => {
  // desktop setup code here...
});
```

### .revert() Method

#### Description
Reverts all animations and ScrollTriggers created in any of the associated MatchMedia functions.

#### Syntax
```js
mm.revert();
```

#### Example
```js
mm.revert();
```

### Conditions Object

Instead of a single media query string, you can pass an object with multiple named queries. The handler function receives an object with boolean properties indicating whether each query matches.

#### Example
```js
mm.add({
  isDesktop: "(min-width: 800px)",
  isMobile: "(max-width: 799px)"
}, (context) => {
  if (context.conditions.isDesktop) {
    // desktop code
  }
  if (context.conditions.isMobile) {
    // mobile code
  }
});
```

### Scope

The optional `scope` parameter scopes all GSAP selector text inside the handler to a specific element or ref. This is useful for component-based frameworks like React or Angular.

#### Example
```js
mm.add("(min-width: 800px)", () => {
  gsap.to(".box", { x: 100 }); // only selects .box inside the scope element
}, myElementOrRef);
```

### Cleanup Function

The handler function can optionally return a cleanup function that runs when the media query stops matching.

#### Example
```js
mm.add("(min-width: 800px)", () => {
  // setup code
  return () => {
    // custom cleanup code
  };
});
```

### Simplistic Desktop/Mobile Example
```js
let mm = gsap.matchMedia();

mm.add("(min-width: 800px)", () => {
  // desktop setup code here...
});

mm.add("(max-width: 799px)", () => {
  // mobile setup code here...
});
```
```

